<nav class="seg topseg" aria-label="About"><a href="/about/how">How it is made</a><a class="on" href="/about/formats" aria-current="page">Formats</a></nav><!-- aria-current: round fifty-eight; the accessibility review -->

<article class="prose">
  <h2 id="log">The change log</h2>
  <p>A collection is a list of changes. Each change sets one field of one record:</p>
<!-- A block that scrolls sideways is reachable and scrollable by keyboard, and named (round fifty-eight; the accessibility review). -->
<!-- svelte-ignore a11y_no_noninteractive_tabindex -->
<pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="An example change"><code>{`{ "t": "1789520000000-0001-a7f3c2c9d1e4x0z9", "kind": "accession", "id": "rmu3e054w01a7f3c2c9d1e4x0z9", "field": "notes", "value": "sulked all summer" }`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>t</code> is a hybrid logical clock: 13-digit millisecond wall time, a hex counter of four digits, or six: past 65,535 changes in one millisecond, or for an edit placed past another stamp, which sets the bit 0x800000 (below), and the writer: the 12-character id of the device that made the change followed by a 4-character tag for the browser tab (each tab of one device is its own clock), 16 characters in all; a change written by the app's own number repair carries a writer starting <code>zz</code> instead. It orders by wall time, then counter value, then writer. To find one device's changes, match the first 12 characters of the writer against the backup manifest's <code>device</code>, not the whole. A device follows a peer's clock only up to five minutes ahead of its own, so one phone set to the wrong year cannot become every device's clock. <code>kind</code> is one of <code>accession</code>, <code>event</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>sowing</code> (what the app calls a propagation batch; a <code>location</code> is a place), <code>photo</code>, <code>taxon</code>, <code>setting</code>. A delete is a change setting <code>_deleted</code> to true; a later change to any other field of that record revives it. The fields <code>id</code>, <code>kind</code> and <code>_t</code> belong to the record and cannot be set by a change (nor can the names <code>*</code> and <code>_deleted=</code>).</p>
  <p>To read a collection, sort the changes by <code>t</code> and apply them in order, keeping for each record and field the value with the greatest <code>t</code>. The result is the same whatever order the changes arrived in, and applying a change twice changes nothing. That is the merge rule; backup restore and sync both use it, with four refinements a reader who wants the same collection must follow too: two changes under one stamp (which a correct writer never mints) keep the one whose value sorts greater, and for a removal the removal; a record is live only while it has its required fields (a plant its name and status, a batch its count), so a record arriving field by field is shown only once whole; a record is removed if its latest removal is later than its latest edit to any other field, so an edit after a removal brings it back; and the hold and the parking rules below, which keep a change from a wrong clock out of the fold for a while or for good.</p>
  <p>Record shapes are plain JSON with flat fields. Records are keyed by an opaque identity (<code>id</code>, minted once on the device that made the record and never shown: a kind prefix, <code>r</code> for a plant, <code>s</code> for a batch, <code>l</code> for a place, <code>p</code> for a photograph, <code>e</code> for an event, then the minting stamp's wall time as exactly 8 base-36 digits (the app reads the time as the 8 characters after the prefix), its counter in two or more base-36 digits, and the writer, so an id looks like <code>rmu3e054w01a7f3c2c9d1e4x0z9</code>); the number a plant is known by is a field, <code>acc</code>, unique among live and dead plants and never reused, so two devices that mint the same number offline end up with two plants under one number, never one plant made of both, until the grower renumbers one. A merge (a restore from a file, a sync pull) writes what arrived and nothing more, and never renumbers anything: two records it leaves under one number are both listed, each record's page names the other and says which would keep the number (the one recorded first, by its first stamp), and nothing is renumbered until the grower presses Renumber. While a number is shared, every link the app makes to either record is by its record id, not by the number, and an address by the bare number (a typed one, an old bookmark) opens a chooser listing both. A removed plant or batch brought back (Undo, or Restore on its page) yields its number only to a record that reached this device while it was removed, judged by the order in which changes arrived here and never by two devices' clocks, whenever that record was made: the one coming back then takes the next free number, with a note saying so, and the other keeps its number and its printed label. A change stored before this device kept that order (an older version of the app did not) has no place in it, and a removal of that age comes before anything that has one. When neither the removal nor the other record has a place, which came first is not known: the record that stayed keeps the number, since its label is the one likely printed, and the one coming back takes the next free number, with a note and a message saying the order is not known. A replace from a backup is the same: the file carries no order of arrival, so the changes it wrote have no place in it, and a change that arrives after the replace comes after them all. Opening the collection, or a sync run that brings nothing, reads the log and never writes to it; nor does a page that reads the reference (a plant whose GBIF key the reference does not file its name under says so, and the key changes only when the grower takes the reference's). Sowings carry their batch number in <code>no</code> the same way. <b>Joins are by id, never by number:</b> an event's <code>acc</code> and a photo's <code>acc</code> hold the plant's record id (the <code>rmu3e…</code>, not the <code>2026-0001</code>), and for a sowing's own events (<code>germinate</code>, <code>potup</code>, <code>loss</code>) the sowing's record id; a photo of a batch carries the sowing's id in <code>sowing</code> instead. To list a plant's events, take the plant's <code>id</code> and select the events whose <code>acc</code> equals it.</p>
  <p>The fields, by kind. <b>accession</b>: <code>acc</code>, <code>taxonName</code>, <code>taxonKey</code> (GBIF), <code>nameAsReceived</code>, <code>cultivar</code>, <code>nameKind</code> (species, cultivar or hybrid), <code>parentage</code>, <code>fieldNumber</code>, <code>provenance</code> (wild, f1, fn, veg, unknown), <code>status</code> (growing, archived, dead), <code>locationId</code>, <code>acquired</code> (YYYY-MM-DD, or YYYY-MM or YYYY when an import read only a month or a year), <code>sourceFrom</code>, <code>sourceRef</code>, <code>sourceForm</code>, <code>price</code>, <code>notes</code>, <code>notesBase</code> (the stamp of the notes change the latest notes edit was made from, so an edit made in sight of a text is told from one made blind to it; a text an edit replaced blind stays in the log, and the record's page reads it from there and shows it under the notes, on every device, writing nothing), <code>sowingId</code>, <code>cover</code> (a photo id), <code>waterDays</code> (the plant's own watering rhythm, 1 to 365 days, over its place's; it does not take the plant out of its place's dry months), <code>importKey</code> (the sheet line an import filed the plant from: a 53-bit hash, in base 36 after an "i", of the line's cells as the import reads them, each run of spaces made one and the case folded, then "#" and the plant's place among the plants that line and its identical lines make; nothing of the line's text is kept; absent on a plant not imported). <b>event</b>: <code>acc</code> (a record id, as above), <code>d</code> (YYYY-MM-DD; a plant's acquire line carries its date as read, so YYYY-MM or YYYY after an import), <code>t</code> (acquire, repot, water, feed, treat, prune, flower, pollinate, seed, graft, measure, move, note, death, propagate, audit, germinate, potup, loss), and as the type needs <code>note</code>, <code>cause</code>, <code>used</code>, <code>followUp</code> (days), <code>measures</code> (an object of named finite numbers: diam, h, spread, caudex and, on a repot line, pot in millimetres whatever the display; heads and leaves are counts), <code>n</code> (a count: seedlings up so far, plants potted, plants lost), <code>plants</code> (on a potup line, the ids of the plants it made, so the line names them by their current numbers), <code>auto</code> (true on a line the app wrote by itself, a rename's note or a move when a place is removed, which never counts as the plant being seen; a watering the grower started for a whole place, from Today or the place's page, is the grower's and counts). <b>photo</b>: <code>acc</code> or <code>sowing</code>, <code>d</code>, <code>dFrom</code> (exif or added), <code>caption</code>, <code>w</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>bytes</code>, <code>sha</code> (SHA-256 of the full-size JPEG, hex); its pixels are stored separately under the same id. <b>location</b>: <code>name</code>, <code>parentId</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>indoor</code>, <code>floorC</code>, <code>floorHeld</code> (true when the floor is a heater's set-point rather than what the space bottoms out at), <code>ppfd</code>, <code>lightHours</code>, <code>lat</code>, <code>lon</code>, <code>altM</code>, <code>waterDays</code> (water about every this many days, 1 to 365: what "due" means for the plants here; 21 when neither the plant nor any place above it sets one), <code>dryMonths</code> (an array of months, 1 to 12, the plants here are kept dry and not due; both inherited down the tree when null, the nearest place that sets one deciding, so an empty list sets no dry months over a parent's), <code>notes</code>, <code>sort</code>; temperatures are stored in °C whatever the display. <b>sowing</b>: <code>no</code>, <code>taxonName</code>, <code>taxonKey</code>, <code>cultivar</code>, <code>nameKind</code>, <code>parentage</code>, <code>method</code>, <code>parentAcc</code> (a plant's record id), <code>sown</code> (YYYY-MM-DD), <code>count</code>, <code>sourceFrom</code>, <code>sourceRef</code> (the seller's lot), <code>fieldNumber</code> (the collector's number, which the plants potted up inherit), <code>provenance</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>container</code>, <code>treatment</code>, <code>bottomHeatC</code>, <code>covered</code>, <code>locationId</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>notes</code>, <code>notesBase</code>. <b>taxon</b> (keyed by the species slug): <code>name</code>, <code>gbifKey</code>, <code>myNotes</code>, <code>myNotesBase</code> (the species' own notes' base, as a plant's notes have one: a text replaced blind is read from the log and shown under them), <code>followed</code>. <b>setting</b>: one record, id <code>numbering</code>, with <code>scheme</code>, the numbering scheme every device shares.</p>

  <h2 id="backup">The backup file</h2>
  <p><code>cultifolio-YYYY-MM-DD.cultifolio.zip</code>, an ordinary zip:</p>
<!-- svelte-ignore a11y_no_noninteractive_tabindex -->
<pre tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="What the backup file holds"><code>{`manifest.json        { format: "cultifolio-backup", v: 1, exported: <ISO time>, device, app,
                       counts: { changes, accessions, events, locations, sowings, taxa, photos, photoBytes },
                       photosMissing: [ids of photo records whose pixels were not on the exporting device; absent when none],
                       parked: [stamps of the changes the exporting device had parked; absent when none] }
                       (photos counts the photographs actually in the file; the numbering scheme is a setting record in the log)
changes.json         a JSON array of changes, the whole log, as above
photos/<id>.jpg      full-size JPEG, long edge 1600 px
photos/<id>.t.jpg    320 px thumbnail
plants.csv           one row per plant, for a spreadsheet, with its record id in the last column; the import reads it back (the id and sowing columns are listed apart, and a line whose id names a plant here is that plant, left out)
batches.csv          one row per propagation batch, with its record id, its latest count, potted and lost figures (not for import)
events.csv           one row per timeline entry, oldest first, with the plant's or batch's record id, number and species (not for import)
device.json          the exporting device's settings { site: { lat, lon, name? } (as entered, not rounded), units, labels, prefs }; a restore applies each only where the device has none`}</code></pre>
  <p>The manifest's <code>v</code> is 2 when the change log holds a marked stamp (below) and 1 otherwise; an older version of the app (one that writes backups only as version 1) refuses a version-2 file with a sentence ("This backup was written by a newer Cultifolio"), rather than restore it and park what every later version shows. Restoring merges by the log rule, so restoring an old file over a newer collection loses nothing and restoring twice changes nothing. The changes the file names as parked are parked here too, before anything is folded; a replace takes the file's parked set in place of the device's. A file names the app that wrote it: this version writes <code>cultifolio 3 (stored parks)</code> and lists only the parks its device stored; a version-1 file naming <code>cultifolio 3</code>, or no app, came from an older version, which listed parks of its clock alone too, so its list is not read and its changes are judged as any change arriving now is. Only the zip is read.</p>

  <h2 id="sync">Sync on the wire</h2>
  <p>One vault key per person: 30 symbols from an alphabet without I, L, O, U, 0 or 1, in six groups of five. From it, HKDF-SHA-256 with salt <code>cultifolio-vault-v1</code> derives three 32-byte keys; the input keying material is the 30 symbols upper-cased with every character outside A–Z and 0–9 removed (dashes, spaces, a line break, and the <code>cultifolio://vault?k=</code> prefix of the pairing link are all dropped, which is what the app does to whatever is typed or scanned): the UTF-8 bytes of that 30-character string, not a decoding of it: an AES-256-GCM key (info <code>enc</code>), a 256-bit auth token (info <code>auth</code>, sent as 64 hex digits in a bearer header) and an HMAC-SHA-256 key for naming log batches (info <code>name</code>). A log batch here is what the sync page calls a sync bundle: one device's sealed bundle of changes. The vault id is 26 symbols made from SHA-256 of the string <code>id:</code> + the token's 64 hex digits: take the first 26 bytes of the digest and map each byte <i>b</i> to <code>ALPHABET[b mod 30]</code>, where the alphabet is <code>ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ23456789</code> (the same 30 symbols the key uses, in that order). It is not a base-32 encoding of the digest. The server stores SHA-256 of <code>token:</code> + token and compares; at creation, the one moment it sees the token itself, it also checks that the id is the one that token derives, so a vault cannot be made under a name its key would not produce.</p>
  <p>A sealed blob is one version byte (1), a 12-byte IV, then AES-GCM ciphertext (a 128-bit tag, appended as WebCrypto does) with associated data the UTF-8 bytes of <code>vaultId|kind</code>, where kind is <code>log</code> or <code>photo</code>, so a blob cannot be replayed into another vault or as another kind; a photo's data also carries its id (<code>vaultId|photo|photoId</code>), so it cannot be handed out under another photo's id. A log batch's data likewise carries its name on the server (<code>vaultId|log|batchName</code>, the name without <code>.bin</code>), so a log batch cannot be posted again under a fresh name and opened there. A blob opens under its own binding and no other. A log batch decrypts to <code>{`{ "v": 1, "device": "...", "changes": [...] }`}</code>, or <code>"v": 2</code>, the same shape, when any of its changes carries the mark (below) or when it carries <code>"parked": [stamps]</code>, the stamps among its changes its sender holds as parked (below). This version reads both. An older version of the app (one that writes log batches only as version 1) sets aside any log batch whose version is not 1, whole and under its name, for a later version to read, so a version that would read the mark as an ordinary counter, or would not read <code>parked</code>, waits rather than folds those changes differently; a log batch of a version this build does not know is set aside the same way. It is stored at <code>vault/&lt;id&gt;/log/&lt;hour&gt;-0000-&lt;device&gt;-&lt;fingerprint&gt;.bin</code> where <code>&lt;hour&gt;</code> is the hour (in ms, padded to 13 digits) of the log batch's last change, <code>&lt;device&gt;</code> the pushing device's 12-character id and <code>&lt;fingerprint&gt;</code> the first twelve hex digits of HMAC-SHA-256, under the vault's naming key, of exactly the bytes JavaScript's <code>JSON.stringify(changes)</code> gives: no spaces, keys in the order each change carries them; a serialiser that adds spaces or sorts keys gives a different fingerprint. Every seal uses a fresh IV, so the same log batch pushed twice is different bytes under the same name; the pushing device sends the full fingerprint (<code>X-Batch-Plain</code>) and its id (<code>X-Device</code>), both required, and when both match what the server kept with the first copy it answers that the log batch is already there and stores nothing. Different bytes under a held name without that match are refused (409), so a name never stands for two contents. The fingerprint is keyed so that the server holds nothing it could test a guessed plaintext against. A log batch the device cannot open or validate is set aside by name and reported, and does not stop the ones behind it; a log batch that cannot be stored on the device (a full phone) is not set aside: the sync stops with an error and fetches it again next time. A change stamped more than five minutes ahead of the receiving device's clock (a peer's, or one in a restored file) is stored but held out of the merge until that clock reaches it, and the plants list and Today say how many are waiting; otherwise one fast phone would win every field it touched on every device. That clock is the device's corrected by the server's: every sync answer carries a <code>Date</code> header, and a device whose clock disagrees with it by more than half a minute stamps its changes, dates its events and judges its holds by the server's time instead; a correction of two days or more is taken only when two readings from two sync runs, a minute or more apart, agree, so one wrong answer cannot move a clock by years. The correction is kept in the browser's storage (<code>localStorage</code>, under <code>cultifolio.clockOffsetMs</code>), is shared with the device's other tabs, and is cleared by Stop syncing. No amount of time passing drops it: a slow clock does not catch up by waiting. Within an open page the device clock is read against the browser's monotonic clock, and a move of more than half a minute between the two is a move of the device clock. A move back (the clock set back, by hand or by a time sync) first undoes the moves forward kept since the last reading (below), as far as they go: the clock set forward by mistake and put back. Beyond that, it is followed only as far as a negative correction says the clock was fast: the correction shrinks by the move, so a fast clock set right needs no reading. A move back further still is kept, not followed: nothing says the clock was that far fast, and a clock left slow stamps behind, which parks nothing. A move forward by about a positive correction is a slow clock set right, and is followed the same way. Any other move forward is a sleep (some platforms pause the monotonic clock while the device sleeps) or the clock set forward; the two cannot be told apart, so the correction is kept, and the move is added to the moves forward kept since the last reading, which are stored with the correction, so a reload and the device's other tabs undo them too. Kept, a clock set forward stamps ahead by the move, and by any positive correction besides, until a reading. After any such move, and at every page load, the correction is kept but unconfirmed, and a reading is asked for at once: a reading that agrees with it to within half a minute confirms it, and the log is not folded again unless the clock had not counted as confirmed before; one that disagrees replaces it, and the log is folded once. Being unconfirmed changes nothing else: changes are stamped and dates read by the correction in force, holds are judged by it, and this device's own changes are never held or parked by its own clock. Offline, a slow device whose clock was set right and whose page was reloaded before any reading stamps its edits by the old correction until the first reading; when that correction was more than two days, their arrival then parks them, on that device too, and the Sync page's "Apply all from this device" writes them again as edits made now. A first reading that disagrees with the clock in force by more than two days stops it counting as confirmed until a second reading agrees; the correction stays in force and stored meanwhile, so every tab and the next page load stamp alike. What is kept is the correction, the device's and the server's time of the reading, whether a reading has met it since the last move or load, and the moves forward kept since the last reading. A reading is trusted only while it is under a week old and not dated more than five minutes after the device's own clock: five minutes of slack cover the reading's rounding and a clock nudged back by a time sync, and a reading further in the future means the clock was set back since, so it confirms nothing, and at a page load the correction it gave is not used. A correction whose last reading is older than a week stays in force, but no change is parked by it alone. A change stamped more than two days past the arrival of the log batch that carried it is parked, on every device including the one that made it, and listed with Apply: it is a broken clock's, kept in the log and never folded on its own, and the record it belongs to offers to apply it as an edit made now, so every device takes it. Each device lists the vault, so it learns when its own log batches arrived, and it judges its own changes by that arrival as it judges a peer's (a log batch of its own that may hold such a stamp is fetched again to be judged; a device that synced before this rule lists the vault from the start once). A change this device made is never held, and is judged once its log batch is listed. No device keeps a verdict of its own clock alone: another device's change with no arrival to judge it by (one read from a file) is held as a peer's is, or, against a clock a sync server has confirmed, parked for that load only and judged again at the next; a change read from a file is parked for good only if the file names it as parked. An edit always wins: it is stamped just past the field's current stamp, however far ahead that stamp is, and such a stamp carries a mark in its counter (the bit 0x800000) saying it was placed after the field's stamp and not read from a clock. A marked stamp is never held or parked, on any device, so an edit made after a clock was put right shows everywhere, and a later edit anywhere is placed past it in turn. A field, and a record's removal, are ordered by a marked stamp's place, which is just past the far stamp it was placed after, until real time reaches that stamp: an edit or a removal made later elsewhere, on a device that had not yet seen the marked edit, sorts before it, even one made on a right clock, so every device shows the marked edit alike; it resolves by the stamps, not by when each was made. The mark says how a stamp was made, and nothing checks it: it is trusted as the key is. A device holding the vault key that marks a far stamp read from its own clock escapes the hold and the park, wins the fields it touches until real time reaches the stamp, and raises no clock line; every later edit is placed past it in turn. An older version of the app, from before the mark, parks such a stamp; a log batch carrying one is version 2, which that version sets aside. A number repair past a marked stamp is placed past it with the mark too. A device still on an older version that renumbers the same plant places its own repair just past that stamp without the mark; when the stamp is a broken clock's, far ahead, this version parks that repair, and leaving it parked keeps one renumbering and one note. No clock counts onto the mark: a counter one short of it moves to the next millisecond, its own or one it follows. A separate record of real time beside the stamp would close this, and is not made. A device whose clock was a few days fast, offline for a month and then put right: its last days of stamps arrive less than two days ahead, so its peers hold them rather than park them. A peer's edit to one of those fields, made meanwhile, shows until the held change comes due, and is then replaced by the older edit, with no notice. Every device ends the same. Two words for the two states: a <b>held</b> change waits for the clock to reach its stamp and then folds by itself; a <b>parked</b> change stays in the log and is never folded unless the grower applies it. A held change that an edit here has since been stamped past will never show, and is not counted as waiting. A parked change that an edit was since stamped past is no longer offered with Apply; Apply takes a record's latest parked removal or restore, by its stamp, and on a restore brings the record back. A new vault is sent the parked changes too, with their verdict: the log batch lists them under <code>parked</code>, and a device reading it stores them as parked before it folds the rest, as it does a file's, so every device parks them alike and offers Apply (judged by the new vault's arrival alone, a change parked long ago would no longer be far ahead, and would fold there). Apply sends one as an edit made now. "Apply all from this device" on the Sync page applies this device's own parked changes at once; a peer's are left for the grower to judge one by one. A device whose clock reads earlier than its own latest change says so on its clock line; that acts on nothing, and edits still save and show. Log batches are handed out in the order they <em>arrived</em> at the server, not in key order: a device that edited offline and uploads after another device has already pulled past that time must still be found, so a client asks for everything since a server arrival time, re-reads a minute of overlap, and skips log batches it already holds; within one sync it pages on the pair (arrival, key), so many log batches arriving in the same second cannot repeat. The changes inside carry the HLCs that settle merges; arrival order only decides discovery. A photo decrypts to a 4-byte big-endian length, the full JPEG, then the thumbnail, at <code>vault/&lt;vaultId&gt;/photo/&lt;photoId&gt;.bin</code> (or, stored again after a removal, at a later generation of that name: below). No log batch or photograph is ever written over (a photograph's pointer, below, is); nothing is deleted but a removed photograph's bytes, at a device's request, and bytes an upload cut off left unnamed.</p>
  <p id="snapshot">On the device, the log is what is kept: an IndexedDB store of changes keyed by <code>t</code>, an outbox of the stamps the server has not acknowledged, and the order the changes arrived in (a numbered row per change stored). The merged state the pages read is a reading of the log, and the device keeps the last reading as a snapshot beside it, with the arrival number it was taken at, so a load folds the snapshot and the changes that arrived after it rather than the whole log again. The snapshot is only ever a cache of the log: it is dropped, in the same transaction, whenever the log is replaced from a file, a stored change is displaced by one that ranks higher under its stamp, or a change is parked on its arrival (one the fold parks itself is parked by every fold of the same log, so the snapshot stands); a load that finds the log changed between reading the snapshot and reading what followed it folds the log instead; and it is not read when it was built under other fold rules (a deploy that leaves the rules as they were keeps it), by another device, or under another clock correction or with the clock confirmed where it is now not, or the reverse (every hold was judged by the old clock, and whether it was confirmed decided which verdicts were kept). What the snapshot did not fold it names, every stamp: the held changes are judged again at each load and folded as they come due, and the parked ones are read back so their Apply stands. A backup carries the log, never the snapshot; a sync sends the log, never the snapshot; a device with no snapshot folds the log and is the same collection. The database is at version 4; opening it at a newer version tells a tab still running an older version of the app, which lets go of it once its writes in flight have landed and reloads into the newer one, so a tab left open across an update does not go on writing beside it.</p>
  <p id="sample">The sample collection is a database of its own on the device (IndexedDB <code>cultifolio-demo</code>, beside the grower's <code>cultifolio</code>), chosen when a page loads by a flag in this tab's session storage (<code>cultifolio.demo</code>), so entering or leaving it reloads the page. Nothing of the sample reaches the grower's own log, settings, a backup or sync, and leaving it deletes its database whole: Leave goes to your own collection first and the first page there deletes the sample, so a page that asks "Leave site?" (an unsaved form) and is answered Cancel stays a working sample tab, and that Leave is called off, so a later reload or closing the tab keeps the sample, and the Leave button comes back; when you added or changed records in the sample, Leave says how many and asks first; a deletion held up because another tab still has the sample open is said, and finishes when that tab closes. Its settings are its own: in the sample, units, the label sheet, preferences, the site, the last places chosen, the species compared and the plants picked for labels are kept for that tab only, in its session storage under <code>cultifolio.demo.</code> and the key's name (<code>cultifolio.demo.units</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.labels</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.prefs</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.frost.site</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.theme</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.lastLocation</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.lastSowLocation</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.countMode</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.compare</code>, <code>cultifolio.demo.labelsPicked</code>), no cookie is written from it, and Settings, Sync and Backup are shut there; a restore refuses to start in it, and so does sync, in the code and not only on the page. It is set out in one change. Each tab in the sample holds a browser lock named <code>cultifolio-sample-open</code>; a page outside the sample deletes a sample database that no tab holds, so one left by a tab closed without leaving does not come back stale. A browser without these locks cannot tell an open sample from a left one, so there the leftover is kept rather than risk deleting one open in another tab. Leaving tells the sample's other tabs, on a channel named <code>cultifolio-sample</code>, and they go to your own collection and say why; a tab that learns it from the database instead does the same.</p>
  <p>The endpoints: <code>POST /api/sync/vault</code> {`{ id, token, create }`}; <code>GET /api/sync/log?vault=&amp;since=&lt;ms&gt;</code> (returns <code>{`{ batches: [{ key, at }], more, next?: { at, key } }`}</code>; the next page is <code>&amp;after=&lt;at&gt;:&lt;key&gt;</code>); <code>POST /api/sync/log?vault=</code> with headers <code>X-Batch</code>, <code>X-Batch-Plain</code>, <code>X-Device</code>; <code>GET /api/sync/log/&lt;hour&gt;-0000-&lt;device&gt;-&lt;fingerprint&gt;?vault=</code> (the log batch name, as above, without the <code>.bin</code>); <code>PUT|GET|HEAD|DELETE /api/sync/photo/&lt;id&gt;?vault=</code> (DELETE: a device that has folded the photograph's removal, ten minutes on, asks for its bytes to go; they come off the vault's count, and a 404 is taken as done. The PUT carries <code>X-Photo-Drop</code>, the HMAC-SHA-256 of <code>drop:&lt;id&gt;</code> under the vault's naming key, which the server keeps with the object; the DELETE must repeat it, or is refused with 403, so the token alone can add to a vault and never destroy in it). All but creation take <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>. Every request the sync engine sends carries <code>X-Cultifolio-Sync: 1</code>. A write with <code>Origin: null</code> and no <code>Sec-Fetch-Site</code>, which is what a browser from before <code>Sec-Fetch-Site</code> (Safari before 16.4) sends for this site's own requests under its no-referrer policy, is taken as the site's own only when it carries that header; any other write that names another site is refused (403). A page on another site cannot send the header without asking first, and this site never says yes. A body larger than the limit is 413; a vault with no room left (2 GB) is 507 with <code>{`{ error: "vault full", bytes, limit }`}</code>, and the device stops sending and says so rather than splitting the log batch. A refusal the sync code makes itself is JSON with an <code>error</code>; one the framework makes (a bad vault id, a missing token, a body past the limit) is JSON with a <code>message</code>, and a fetch of a log batch or photograph that is not there is a plain-text 404; the device shows whichever of the two fields the body carries. One address (an IPv4 address, or an IPv6 /64) is bounded: 5 new vaults and 3 GB stored per day, 600 requests per ten minutes to open, list and push and 3,000 to fetch or store single log batches and photographs; past any of these the answer is 429 with <code>Retry-After</code> in seconds, and the device simply tries again later. For an IPv6 address the /48 around it is counted as well: 20 new vaults a day, and requests for opening, listing and pushing, for single log batches and photographs, and for the reference's index and sheets. New vaults are also bounded for everyone. A new vault takes its place among the day's new vaults (200) and among the vaults the site holds in all (2,000) when its first log batch or photograph is stored, not when it is made, so a vault made and never used takes neither. A vault keeps its place while it is used. If its first upload does not land, the place is given back, unless another upload of it is still landing. A vault with no upload for 90 days gives its place back at the next midnight UTC. The server first writes the time into the vault's own record (<code>reclaimedAt</code> in its <code>meta.json</code>), then gives the place back. Nothing the vault holds is touched, and every device can still read it, but reading keeps no place. Its next upload takes a place again among the 2,000, not among the day's new vaults, and only once that place is checked: if the counters cannot be read, the upload is refused with 503 and <code>Retry-After: 60</code>; if no place is free, with 503, <code>Retry-After: 3600</code> and a sentence that says so ("Sync is full for now. This vault had no upload for 90 days, so its place was given back. …"). A vault made by an older version of the server (counted when it was made, or when it was first filled, with no place recorded) is recorded at its next upload and not counted again. When the day's places are taken, a vault that holds nothing yet has its uploads refused until midnight UTC; when the places in all are taken, until more are added. Past either ceiling the answer is 503 with <code>Retry-After</code> and a sentence the sync page shows. Joining a vault never takes a place; a joined vault that holds nothing yet is counted at its first upload like any other. A device given that answer keeps receiving, sends nothing more until the <code>Retry-After</code> has passed or within the hour, whichever comes first, and the sync page shows the server's own sentence; the refusal is kept with the vault's sync state on the device, so a reload or another tab waits too. A 503 asking for less than a minute (a photograph another device is storing, a count that crossed an upload) is not kept: the device tries again then. A photograph another device is storing or removing is answered with <code>Retry-After: 10</code>, whatever is left of that device's hold, so one photograph never holds up the vault's other uploads. A 503 with no <code>Retry-After</code> is taken as five minutes and kept like any other refusal, as "The sync server is not taking uploads from this vault just now." when it carries no sentence. An upload that would make a vault's first object is also 503, with <code>Retry-After: 60</code>, when the counters cannot be read at all: an uncounted vault is a vault the ceilings never saw, so it is refused rather than stored ("The site could not count this vault just now; try again in a minute."). A listing of a vault holding more log batches than one listing can walk is 503 too. The byte counts live in a counter object (a Durable Object), one per vault and one per address: each upload's bytes are checked against the allowance and taken in one step, so two uploads in the same instant cannot both pass on the same figure; an upload not yet written is held as a lease on its bytes, released when it lands or fails and lapsing by itself after ten minutes, so an upload whose Worker stopped mid-way cannot hold its bytes for good; a removal is held the same way, with no bytes, from just before it deletes until its bytes are given back; a photograph being stored and a removal of the same photograph wait for each other (a 503 with a short wait, which the device retries), and a removal made before an upload of that photograph claimed it leaves the photograph in place (409; the DELETE carries the removal's time in <code>X-Photo-Removed-At</code>). An upload claims a photograph's name for two days, and only an upload that carries the photograph's removal proof claims it, whatever it is answered (stored, already there, or 409 for different bytes, which is what an Undo sealed again sends); so a first upload that lands after another device removed the record is removed once its claim is two days old. The proof is checked before anything else. A device answered 409 does not take the removal as done: it asks again at a later sync, at most once an hour (the time it was last answered is kept in the sync record, so a reload or another tab waits too), until the server removes the bytes (a success or 404) or the photograph is live again; a 403 (a proof that does not match the upload's) is said once on the sync page and not asked again. A photograph is stored by generation. Its first is at <code>vault/&lt;vaultId&gt;/photo/&lt;photoId&gt;.bin</code>; a photograph stored again after its removal is a new generation, at <code>vault/&lt;vaultId&gt;/photo/&lt;photoId&gt;.g</code> and base-36 digits, named by a pointer at <code>vault/&lt;vaultId&gt;/photoref/&lt;photoId&gt;.json</code>: <code>{`{ g }`}</code> (the generation that holds it; <code>bin</code> for the first), or after a removal <code>{`{ g: null, drop, at, was }`}</code>, the removal's receipt (its proof, its time and the generation it removes), each with a random <code>n</code> that makes every write of it its own. A removal renews its hold, moves the pointer to its receipt only if the pointer is still the one it read (the bucket's own conditional write), and only then deletes the generation it looked at, so a removal that stalls anywhere can remove nothing but that generation. An upload with the proof that finds the photograph already there (a retry after a lost reply, or an Undo) moves the pointer too, naming the generation it found, so a removal that read the pointer before it and stalled past its hold finds it moved, removes nothing and is answered 409; if a stalled removal's pointer write lands first, the upload is asked to wait ten seconds and then stores the photograph again as a new generation. A removal cut off after its receipt was written (the bucket's delete failed, or the server stopped) is finished when the device asks again: the receipt names the generation, a request with the removal's proof deletes it and gives its bytes back, and is answered as a removal made, so the 404 a device takes as done comes only when nothing is left. A photograph whose name holds a receipt is stored again only with the proof the receipt holds (403 otherwise), so the token alone cannot put back a copy of a removed photograph. Photographs stored before the server kept generations stay where they are, as their name's first generation. The pointers are not counted against the vault's 2 GB. Bytes that nothing names (an upload cut off between storing a new generation and naming it, or a first generation left behind after a removal) are removed once they are ten minutes old at the next touch of that photograph: its next removal, its next storing, or an upload that finds it already there. Until then they count against the vault's 2 GB. A removed object's bytes are given back once, under its name and version, however often the removal is asked for. A listing of the vault puts its total right once a day, on an open at most once an hour, and at the next request after an upload's lease lapsed, the open leases added to it. It is kept only if no upload landed and no removal was made while it was being taken, and none is still between the bucket and the count (while one is, no listing is made at all); after two tries that end that way, the request is answered 503 with <code>Retry-After: 30</code> and the device tries again (opening or joining a vault waits that out and asks again, at most twice), except that an open of a vault whose count was already put right that day answers that count. The midnight sweep gives places back a page at a time, each page and the lowered count of places in one step, so a sweep cut short loses no place. Without the counter object bound, KV keeps the counts by read and write, which bounds the totals but cannot make them exact; it counts a vault's place once, by a key of its own, and does not reclaim places.</p>
  <p>What the server can see. It holds a vault id, a hash of the token, and sealed blobs, and it cannot open any of them. But the blob names, sizes and arrival times are in the clear: a log batch is named by the hour and device id of its last change, a photograph by the time and device id of its creation, and a sealed photograph is its full JPEG plus its 320-pixel thumbnail plus 17 bytes (a 4-byte length, the version byte and the IV) and the 16-byte tag. So the operator can count the devices in a vault, see to the hour when each one made changes and to the second when it synced, count the log batches (roughly, how often changes are made) and the photographs, read their sizes, and see which photographs were removed or stored again. This is not zero-knowledge in the strict sense: the pattern of activity is visible, its content is not. Nothing about the plants themselves (names, notes, places, dates, the number of plants) is visible. Anyone holding the bearer token (which is derived from the key, but could be captured from a device) can add log batches to the vault, which the devices then set aside as unreadable, and can read the ciphertext; the token cannot delete a photograph (its removal needs a proof only a key-holder can make) and cannot read anything.</p>
  <p id="privacy">The reference lookups your own pages make are the other channel. A plant page, a batch page, Today, the labels, the plants list and the front page's own tiles need the reference's figures for your species, and they ask for them by hash bucket, never by name or key: the species slug is hashed (FNV-1a) into one of a number of buckets of roughly three hundred species (32 at the catalogue's present size; the count is a power of two chosen by the corpus build and announced by <code>/api/corpus</code>, so it grows with the catalogue and never past a few hundred species a bucket), the device asks <code>/api/sheets?b=</code> or <code>/api/entries?b=</code> for the buckets it needs, naming the count it hashed by (<code>&amp;n=</code>; a count that is not the one served is refused with a 409 and the device reads the corpus again), and picks its own species out of the answer. A group that cannot be read is answered 503 with a Retry-After of at most thirty seconds, and the server process that refused it does not try that group again for those thirty seconds. The server sees which buckets a device asked for, which narrows each species to one in a few hundred, and no more; a species page you open is, of course, a page you opened; a search typed on the front page or on the compare page, a name typed in the species picker, as typed, and a name in an import that the hash groups do not settle, go to <code>/api/search?q=</code>, and the answer is kept a day in the site's cache under that text, linked to no address, since it is a question about the catalogue and not about your plants, except what is searched on the device and never sent: the text typed on "Your species", anything shaped like a plant number (two to four digits and another, a dash between or not, as 2026-0013; under a prefix scheme, the collection's prefix followed by a dash or a digit, as ACC-0013; or a batch's number, as S2026-001), and anything typed before your collection has opened; and the catalogue's rows you scroll to or jump to by letter are asked for by position (<code>/api/rows</code>: a grouping and a row number, nothing of yours). The service worker keeps those answers under the corpus id (each request carries it, from <code>/api/corpus</code>), so a second visit asks nothing until the corpus is refreshed. The reference's photographs are not fetched for your own pages unless you switch them on in Settings; then they come from the image host directly, which sees which species this address asked a picture of.</p>

  <h2 id="search">Search</h2>
  <p>Search reads a query in turn: whole, against the English common names; then as a botanical name, where <i>var.</i>, <i>v.</i>, <i>variety</i>, <i>subsp.</i>, <i>ssp.</i>, <i>subspecies</i>, <i>f.</i>, <i>fo.</i> and <i>forma</i> are skipped, what follows <i>cv.</i> is a cultivar and is not searched, "cf.", "cfr.", "aff." and "nr." are skipped as "x" is, and so are "vel" before "aff." or "cf." and "near" between two words of a name, and "sp.", "spp." and "nov." end the name; "x" or "×" marks a hybrid, and the word after it starts a name again and is never an author; an author pasted after the name is ignored (a bracket, "&amp;", "ex", a capitalised abbreviation longer than four characters such as "N.E.Br.", and, after an epithet or a rank only, a shorter one such as "L." or any word ending in ".", such as "l." or "mill.", and a capitalised word after an epithet of four letters or more); a quoted text that begins with a capital, after a word of the name, is a cultivar and is not searched; and every English common name a species has is searched, not only the first; a word in a script the search cannot read is left out, and said. The query is NFKC-normalised and loses its format characters (a zero-width space, a soft hyphen) first. When nothing matches exactly, one typing error per word of four letters or more is forgiven (after a capitalised first word, a capitalised word only against a whole word of a name, never the start of one), and then the first two words of the name are searched, before any rank, "sp." or quoted cultivar (a hyphenated epithet is one word; a hybrid formula is searched on its first parent; a single letter is never searched again). Last, a query whose first word is capitalised and whose second begins with a capital is answered with the species of its first word when that word is a genus of the reference (or one typing error from one, at four letters or more), and with nothing otherwise. <code>/api/search?q=</code> answers a list; whenever the answer used other words than those typed (the search on fewer words, the genus answered last, or a reading that left out an author, a cultivar, "sp." or a word it cannot read), the <code>X-Search-Relaxed</code> header carries the words searched, URI-encoded; when a reading matched and left words out (not when the query was searched again), the <code>X-Search-Left</code> header carries those words as typed, URI-encoded; and when the hits came by a forgiven typing error, <code>X-Search-Near: 1</code>; with <code>&amp;shape=2</code> the answer is <code>{`{ hits, relaxed?: { query, left? }, near?: true }`}</code>. <code>/api/names?q=</code> takes letters, marks, spaces, ".", "'", "’", "-", "&amp;" and "×", NFKC-normalised, and answers 400 for anything else (a digit).</p>
  <p>A name picked in the Add form's species picker replaces only what it names: a qualifier, a cross's parentage and a cultivar are kept as typed, and a rank with its epithet is kept (written var., subsp. or f.) when the species picked has the epithet typed; the reference key is filed only when the name filed is the picked taxon's own. When a pick leaves typed words out (an author, a locality), the text as typed is kept as the plant's name as received. A nothogenus keeps its sign (× Gasteraloe), and a field number after the genus (Gymnocalycium LB 123) is kept as written.</p>

  <h2 id="import">Import, the watering calendar and the label code</h2>
  <p><b>Import</b> (My plants, Import) reads a list pasted one plant per line (name; cultivar; number; where it came from; notes, separated by semicolons or tabs), or a CSV sheet with a comma, semicolon or tab separator, quoted cells and an optional byte order mark. It is read on the device, and nothing in the sheet is dropped unseen: cells are kept as text and never evaluated (a cell written <code>="0012"</code>, as the backup's sheets write the shapes of text listed below, is read as <code>0012</code>, and a note keeps its own spaces and line breaks); a column no field takes is listed before the names are checked and, unless you untick it, added to each plant's notes as "Column: value"; Genus and Species in two columns make one name; a name cell is read with its compatibility forms folded (full-width letters, ligatures, a no-break space: Unicode NFKC) and its invisible format characters taken out (a soft hyphen, a zero-width space, a byte order mark; a joiner inside an emoji is kept), and a cell that held a soft hyphen, a zero-width space or full-width letters keeps its text as the plant's name as received; a Qty of 3 is three plants; a part of a name in brackets is kept with the name as received; "cf." (or "cfr."), "aff." (or "vel aff."), "nr." (or "near"), "sp." and "nov." stay in the name, written one way, whether before the genus or after it, and a name in quotes after "sp." is the undescribed species' own; the reference is asked about the species part only, so such a plant is filed with no reference key, and its label's care line says whose care it borrows ("care as for C. cinerea"). A plant filed under a name other than the sheet's (a suggestion used, a name edited in the review, a word not read) keeps the sheet's text as its name as received, and "Use it" puts the suggested species in place of the species part only. A Status or Kind the import cannot read is said on its line and kept in the notes, as Provenance is; a Qty of "3.0" is three plants. Dates are read at the precision they are written at: a year (2009), a month (August 2017, 2017-08), a named month with a day, or a day and month where a number over 12, or the same number twice, says which is which; a two-digit year is read after a day and month, as 20YY, and only when 20YY has come (1/1/27 could be 1927 or 2027, so it is not read), and not in three numbers joined by dashes, or when the first number could be a year too (24/03/09, 24.03.09), which could be written year first. A time after a date (09/03/2024 10:15) is not part of it; a day or month of 0 is no date; a year and a month joined by a full stop (2019.5) is a number, and is not read. A month name and a two-digit number (Aug-17), as a spreadsheet writes a month, is not read, since the number could be the year or the day; nor is a five-digit number (45123), which may be a spreadsheet's count of days: the line says which, with the date it would be. A date like 09/03/2024 is read day first or month first only by one choice for the whole sheet, and left as written unless you make it; the question says what the sheet's other dates show (a first number over 12 can only be day first, a second over 12 only month first), and chooses nothing for you. A date in the future, or before 1900, is not read either. A date not read is said on its line and kept in the notes as "Acquired (as written): …", and its plant is numbered for the year it names, or this year when it names none. A sheet with a quote that is opened and never closed is not read, and the page says on which row. A row repeating the header is left out and counted, as is a line with no name. A place path is split on " › ", after a place whose whole name is the cell; when the sheet's place cells have a ">" between words (Greenhouse > Bench 2, Greenhouse>Bench 2), a "/" with a space on each side (Greenhouse / Bench 2), or a "/" with no spaces between two words of three letters or more after a place here (Greenhouse/Bench 2, with a Greenhouse here), the review asks once whether to read those as paths too, yes by default when the first part names a place here or begins another such cell. Nothing in brackets is a step, so Cold frame (N/S), S/W window and Shelf 1/2 are each one place. A number already used here is replaced by the next free number, a number given twice in the sheet keeps it on its first line, each plant an import adds carries its line's import key, and running the same sheet again leaves out every line whose plants are all here and offers only the missing plants of a line partly added; a line whose number a plant here holds under the same name looks already imported, and is skipped unless you add it anyway when that plant has no import key (added by hand, or by an import before this version), or kept, said and given the next free number when that plant came from another line; the review before anything is saved says each. The numbers shown in the review are the numbers given: the import gives each plant its number, and a number another tab takes meanwhile is replaced by the next free one, said. One import is written at a time in this browser: before writing, it reads what other tabs wrote, leaves out every plant whose import key is already here, and names the lines imported elsewhere, so a sheet imported in two tabs adds each plant once. 2,000 lines are read at a time, the lines already here not counted; the page then offers the next lines of the same file. Lines are written in groups of up to 50 plants, each group one write, so an import cut off partway keeps the groups before it; a group that cannot be written is tried again a line at a time, so the stop names its line. A change to the date order, the notes box, a column, the header or the path question after the names are checked reads the sheet again at once, and says so. A line of several plants bought at one price keeps the price on its first plant, and the others note "Bought with &lt;number&gt;, whose record holds the price."; a failure before the first plant says which places were made. A last watering given for the whole list is one watering line per plant. Names are checked against the reference: by hash group first, then by the catalogue's search for those the groups do not settle; never by GBIF's name service, which the Add form's picker asks. The backup's sheets write <code>="…"</code> around these shapes of text, which a spreadsheet would change into a number or a date: a leading zero (0012), an exponent (1E5), a day and month (3-12, 1/2), a year and month (2026-01), a day, month and year (3-12-2024, 1/2/24) and a run of twelve digits or more; a text that a spreadsheet would read as a formula gets a leading apostrophe, and one that already begins with an apostrophe and a formula sign gets a second. Only those shapes: text such as 3.12, 2026/01, Aug 2017, 1:30 or 50%, which some spreadsheets read as a date, a time or a number, is written as it is. A price is written as typed and never quoted, since quoting it would break other readers; a spreadsheet may show 12.50 as 12.5. In LibreOffice, tick "Evaluate formulas" when opening the sheet, or those cells show as <code>="…"</code>.</p>
  <p><b>The watering calendar</b> is an iCalendar file (<code>.ics</code>, RFC 5545) made on the device: one all-day event per place, and per plant with its own rhythm, repeating every <i>n</i> days (<code>RRULE:FREQ=DAILY;INTERVAL=n</code>) from its next due day, each with a reminder at 09:00 on the day (<code>VALARM</code>, <code>TRIGGER:PT9H</code>; some calendars use their own default instead). Where a place keeps months dry, its rhythm is one series per run of watered months: each ends (<code>UNTIL</code>) on the last day before the dry months, and the next starts on the first day after them, the day Today lists the place's plants again, for two years ahead. A place kept dry in every month makes no event, and the page names it. A rhythm that is not a whole number of days from 1 to 365 (one that came from a backup, an import or a sync) is written at the default of 21 days, and a plant's own at its place's. Every event has a UID that carries a short tag of the collection (a hash of its earliest record's id) and a SEQUENCE that grows with each download, and a series after dry months is named by the first day of its watered months, so a file downloaded again names the same events the same way. Where any series stops at the two-year horizon, the file's last event, on the day the first one stops, says to download the calendar again; when other series stop later or repeat without end, it names the ones that stop that day. A calendar keeps an imported event as it was, so the page still asks for the old Cultifolio events to be deleted before a new file is added.</p>
  <p><b>Spent this year</b> is read from each plant's price where the price is a plain number with at most one currency mark (<code>12</code>, <code>£6</code>, <code>6 EUR</code>, <code>US$12</code>, <code>R$ 30</code>, <code>12 usd</code>), a thousands separator read only where exactly three digits follow it (<code>£1,250</code>, <code>1.234,56 €</code>), by the year the plant was acquired; "free" and "gift" count as nothing spent, and are said apart; any other price is counted as not read, and the page says how many. The names of one currency are one total ($, US$ and USD; £ and GBP; € and EUR; ¥ and JPY; a code in lower case as in capitals); R$, A$, NZ$, C$ and Rs are each their own; prices with no currency are a total of their own; nothing is converted. Today's line "without a photograph in the last twelve months" and the plants list's "No photo in 12 months" read one rule: a growing plant had six months or more, by its acquisition date or else the day its record was made, with no photograph dated in the last twelve months. A date kept as a year or a month counts from its last day ("2026" as 31 December, "2026-04" as 30 April), so a plant is never counted six months old on a day its record does not give; the date itself is kept as written. Both read today's date from the same corrected clock, so they always agree.</p>
  <p><b>The label's QR code</b> is the plant's address, <code>/plants/&lt;identity&gt;#s=&lt;species&gt;&amp;n=&lt;name as printed&gt;</code>. The part after <code>#</code> stays in the phone that scans it and is never sent to the server as written; a phone without that plant shows the species the label names and a link to its page, after asking for that species' hash group (<code>/api/entries?b=</code>, as a plant page asks for its own), so the server learns the group, one species in a few hundred, and not the name. The name in the code is cut to at most 60 code points, by whole characters as a reader sees them (a letter with its marks, a joined emoji), never inside one; every format character (text-direction marks, zero-width spaces and joiners, soft hyphens, tag characters; a joiner inside an emoji is kept) is taken out of it, closing the word up, and a control character or a line or paragraph separator is made a space, a letter keeps at most two marks, and a broken character (a lone surrogate) is replaced, so the label keeps its code; this is done on the label and when a scanned label is shown. A cross has no species page, so its code names no species, and its name is the cross's parents when they are known (Astrophytum asterias × capricorne), as its label prints it; an "sp." plant's code names no species either; a name that cannot be put in a code leaves that one label without its code, and the others keep theirs.</p>

  <h2 id="dossier">A species dossier</h2>
  <p>Each species page is rendered from one JSON document, served at <code>/api/dossier/&lt;gbifKey&gt;</code> (answered <code>no-store</code> and kept by no cache, since a dossier's address stays the same when the corpus is refreshed; a dossier the index lists but that cannot be read just now answers 503, never 404): name and classification, native range as TDWG level-3 codes, open occurrence points with licence tags, the map marker and how it was placed, the climate envelope (median year, 10th and 90th percentile years, the cells and records it rests on, the typical cell) with its sources, extremes, photographs with author and licence, the summary with its licence, literature, links, and an <code>upstream</code> block recording for every source whether it answered, refused, or was carried from an earlier build. GBIF's vernacular names are one row per exact spelling and language, with the first source that gives it (<code>source</code>), the other named sources of that spelling (<code>alsoFrom</code>), how many sources give it when more than one (<code>sources</code>) and GBIF's <code>preferred</code> flag; a list longer than four pages of a thousand rows is kept as far as it was read, and its upstream record says "truncated". The corpus is built from public data under CC0, CC BY and CC BY-SA; the repository's <code>npm run export</code> packages a built corpus as a dataset bundle (the species JSON, <code>species.csv</code>, <code>climate.csv</code>, and the licence terms per source), which is how the reference data leaves the site as data rather than pages.</p>
